{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.server.tsbuildinfo",
    "noEmit": true,

    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "target": "ES2022",

    /* nodenext, NOT bundler. The server is not bundled - `npm run server` hands
       the .ts files straight to Node, which strips the types and runs them as
       real ESM. Under ESM, relative imports need a real file extension, and with
       type-stripping that extension is ".ts" (Node will not rewrite ".js" back to
       ".ts" for you).

       Choosing nodenext here means tsc reports a missing/incorrect extension at
       typecheck time instead of the server dying with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND on
       first boot. That is the whole reason this project is configured separately.

       If the server ever starts importing browser-side modules from src/ (which
       are written bundler-style, without extensions), do NOT paper over it by
       switching this to "bundler" - move the shared code into server-safe files
       with explicit .ts extensions, or the runtime failure comes straight back. */
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,

    /* Node does not COMPILE the TypeScript, it only erases it. Syntax that has
       runtime meaning - `enum`, `namespace`, parameter properties - cannot be
       erased, and Node throws on it at load time.

       This flag makes tsc reject that syntax in the server project, so the
       failure shows up in `npm run typecheck` rather than when the container
       starts. Use a union of string literals instead of an enum, and a plain
       object instead of a namespace. (Requires TypeScript >= 5.8; 5.9 is
       installed.) */
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,

    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["server/**/*.ts"]
}
